    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530408/tenure-track-assistant-professor-plastics-engineering": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor - Plastics Engineering",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor - Plastics Engineering Apply now Job no: 530408 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Plastics Engineering Salary: Anticipate $115,000 - $122,000 Applications Open: Oct 27 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled General Summary of Position: The University of Massachusetts Lowell (UMass Lowell) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor faculty position in Plastics Engineering beginning in September 2027. Located 25 miles northwest of Boston, Massachusetts, the internationally recognized Department of Plastics Engineering at the University of Massachusetts Lowell is the largest ABET accredited plastics engineering program in the Unites States. We seek collaborative scholars pursuing innovative, high-impact research in Advanced Design and Manufacturing of Polymeric Materials. The development and implementation of machine learning (ML) and artificial intelligence (AI) tools in product/machine design and optimization/automation of manufacturing processes is an important part of this position’s responsibilities. The successful candidate is expected to build a strong, externally funded research program, advise undergraduate and graduate students, teach Plastics Engineering courses at undergraduate and graduate levels, and participate in service to the Department, University and profession. Applications are welcome with research and development expertise in one or more of the following areas: AI powered and generative design of polymeric materials, composites, products, machines, and/or molds Design for circularity including life cycle analysis, design with sustainable materials, and design for remanufacture, recycle, repair, and reuse Smart manufacturing including digital twins, AI-driven process automation and control, IoT enabled machines, and cloud manufacturing Advanced manufacturing processes including additive manufacturing, reactive extrusion/molding, micro/nano-scale fabrication, and hybrid manufacturing methods The successful candidate is expected to teach both undergraduate and graduate courses in polymer design and processing areas such as Product and Process Mold Design, Mold Engineering, Plastics Process Engineering, and Processing Theories. The Department of Plastics Engineering values interdisciplinary collaboration, academic excellence, innovation, and student success. Faculty engage in collaborative research across the university and with national laboratories, industry, and other academic institutions, providing opportunities for interdisciplinary research and education. The University of Massachusetts Lowell is a nationally recognized Carnegie R1 research university located 25 miles northwest of Boston in Massachusetts’ high-tech corridor. With more than 17,000 students and over $120 million in sponsored research annually, UMass Lowell provides a dynamic environment for research, education, and interdisciplinary collaboration. The Wall Street Journal ranks UMass Lowell as the No. 1 public university in Massachusetts and the top public institution in the state for social mobility. The university is committed to research excellence, student success, community partnership, and a positive and inclusive campus culture. Minimum Qualifications: Earned doctorate in Plastics, Polymer, Materials, Chemical, or Mechanical Engineering, or a closely related area by the time of appointment Outstanding research record in area(s) of expertise Strong communications skills Commitment to excellence in teaching The ability to work effectively with diverse groups Additional Considerations: Successful candidates will have a record of publications that demonstrate potential success as a researcher; teaching experience is beneficial, as well as experience as a Postdoctoral Fellow. While not required, industrial experience and a record of interdisciplinary collaboration will be viewed positively. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited, and in the Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA union. Although the union contract minimum is set at $75,000, we anticipate a hiring range of $115,000 - $122,000. Initial review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. However, the position may close once an adequate number of qualified applications is received. Please include the following documents with your application: Cover letter Curriculum vitae including list of publications, patents, and other scholarly work Research statement Teaching statement Inclusivity statement Names and contact information of three references will be required during the application process Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530425/assistant-teaching-professor-in-biomedical-engineering": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Biomedical Engineering",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Biomedical Engineering Apply now Job no: 530425 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Biomedical Engineering Salary: Anticipate $ 100,000-$120,000 Applications Open: Oct 27 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled General Summary of Position: The Francis College of Engineering (FCOE) at the University of Massachusetts Lowell (UMass Lowell) invites applications from exceptional candidates for a full-time Assistant Teaching Professor position in the Biomedical Engineering Department. The role is expected to start in September 2027. Teaching Professors play a vital role in developing and delivering curriculum within the FCOE and are actively involved in departmental, college, and university activities. Assistant Teaching Professors enjoy full faculty privileges and undergo an assessment for promotion to Associate Teaching Professor (with equivalent to tenure). The successful candidate will join the department and college faculty and will be responsible for teaching undergraduate and/or graduate courses, engaging in curriculum development, contributing to professional activities, and participating in relevant departmental and university service, including outreach. Teaching faculty play a crucial role in promoting the department’s educational mission, engaging students in experiential learning, and fostering a diverse and inclusive academic community. UMass Lowell’s Department of Biomedical Engineering trains students to turn discoveries into real-world impact in the medical device and biotechnology industries through an ABET-accredited B.S. program—Massachusetts’ first at a public university—with specialized tracks in medical device development, cell and tissue engineering, and biomechanics. Students can accelerate their education with a 4+1 B.S.–to–M.S. option and pursue advanced studies through the intercampus Biomedical Engineering and Biotechnology (BMEBT) M.S./Ph.D. or the Ph.D. in Biomedical Engineering, all enhanced by hands-on research in state-of-the-art labs and core research facilities. Our collaborative discipline-specific and education-based research is supported by NSF, NIH, DoW, and industry, fostering a culture of innovative education and scholarship. UMass Lowell is a nationally recognized public research university that is rapidly growing. The WSJ ranks UMass Lowell as the top public university in Massachusetts and the best in the state for both “Best Value” and social mobility. We are a Carnegie R1 research university, and our students benefit from the UML Guarantee—every first-time, first-year student can participate in at least one paid, credit-bearing, or career-connected experience. Our inclusive campus has received the HEED Award six times, and UMass Lowell is the Commonwealth’s only AASHE STARS Platinum institution—one of just a few worldwide. In addition to strong graduate outcomes, 95% of graduates find their next step within months. These awards reflect a culture that combines research excellence with a focus on student success. Within the FCOE, faculty are part of an ABET-accredited, industry-engaged community that includes biomedical, chemical, civil, environmental, electrical, plastics, mechanical, and computer engineering. Our college has more than 3,000 undergraduate and graduate students, with 20 percent identifying as female and 25 percent as underrepresented minorities in engineering. Collaboration is central to our research environment: the NERVE Center advances robotics and human–robot interaction; the Fabric Discovery Center links advanced textiles to health and defense; the Raytheon–UMass Lowell Research Institute (RURI) and Printed Electronics Research Collaborative (PERC) drive the development of next-generation electronics; and our campus-wide Core Research Facilities provide open access to over 100 instruments and expert staff. Hands-on spaces like the Lin MakerSpace and entrepreneurship programs such as DifferenceMaker foster discovery and real-world impact. At its core, we are a university dedicated to student success and growth. Minimum Qualifications: Doctoral degree in Biomedical Engineering or a closely related discipline earned by the start of the appointment. Ability to teach a range of undergraduate courses in biomedical engineering, including Bioinstrumentation, Bioinstrumentation Lab, Quantitative Physiology, and Quantitative Physiology Lab. Collaborative nature and ability to work effectively with diverse groups. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, both oral and written. Preferred qualifications: Candidates with experience in engineering education and designing effective, inclusive pedagogical approaches will be given strong consideration. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited and in the MTA/Mass. Society of Professors union. Although the union contract minimum is set at $75,000, we anticipate a hiring range of $110,000-$120,000. Initial review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. The position may close once an adequate number of qualified candidates have been received. Please include the following required documents with your application: Curriculum vitae Cover letter Statement of teaching interests and philosophy Inclusivity statement Evidence of teaching effectiveness, including teaching evaluations (optional) Statement of service activities and interests (optional) Names and contact information for three professional references (please do not send recommendation letters) All materials should be submitted electronically through the UMass Lowell employment portal. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530410/clinical-faculty-open-rank-solomont-school-of-nursing": {
      "title": "Clinical Faculty (Open Rank) - Solomont School of Nursing",
      "description": "Clinical Faculty (Open Rank) - Solomont School of Nursing Apply now Job no: 530410 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Solomont School of Nursing Salary: See posting section for salary details (based on rank/qualifications) Applications Open: Oct 28 2026 Applications Close: General Summary of Position: The Susan and Alan Solomont School of Nursing (SSON) at the University of Massachusetts Lowell seeks a full-time, non-tenure track clinical faculty member who is a family nurse practitioner (FNP). This is an open rank position at either the assistant, associate, or full clinical professor rank based on experience in higher education. This position requires an on-campus presence. We seek applicants recognized for teaching expertise, clinical scholarship, ability to work with diverse populations, and excellent oral and written communication skills in English. This is a 9-month, full-time benefited position. A growing, dynamic, and well-regarded school established in 1968, the Solomont School of Nursing enrolls over 550 nursing students in BS, RN to BS, Master's, Doctor of Nursing Practice, and PhD in Nursing programs and has 20 full-time faculty members. NCLEX-RN pass rates and NP certification exam pass rates consistently exceed 90%. The School's diverse research portfolio includes research in health disparities, correctional worker health, occupational health, pediatrics, and women's health. Clinical faculty are expected to teach 9 credits per semester. Faculty in the School often teach across programs, including in the undergraduate and graduate programs. The SSON has two tracks in the nurse practitioner program, including family nurse practitioner and adult gerontologic primary care. Faculty also participate in the supervision of Doctor of Nursing Practice (DNP) projects. All faculty in the School are expected to engage in service to the School, College, and University, commensurate with rank. We seek applicants recognized for clinical practice experience in primary care and outpatient ambulatory settings and experience working with diverse populations of patients and students. The candidate may be appointed at the Assistant, Associate or Full Professor level, depending upon qualifications. The Solomont School of Nursing is located within the Zuckerberg College of Health Sciences (ZCHS), a vibrant, welcoming, and supportive community of 68 faculty and over 2000 students. The ZCHS prepares professionals and scientists in a wide range of health science fields including biomedical, pharmaceutical and nutritional sciences, physical therapy and exercise science, and public health. Faculty research within ZCHS ranges from laboratory research to clinical interventions and community-based projects and there is a supportive culture of interdisciplinary research across the College. Research programs with significant funding and vibrant community partnerships and engagement opportunities enrich and support our educational programs. The College is a leader in interprofessional education, research and practice opportunities for faculty and students. The University of Massachusetts Lowell, located about 30 miles northwest of Boston in the high-tech corridor of Massachusetts, is a diverse public research university enrolling nearly 17,000 students. Approximately 48% of undergraduates identify as being from diverse backgrounds and 43% are first-generation college students. UMass Lowell has experienced dramatic growth in research funding, total endowments, and student enrollment over the past ten years, and was recently ranked the #1 public university in Massachusetts by the Wall Street Journal for the second time, and the #1 public university in New England for return on investment. It is a Carnegie Community-Engaged university, and a Carnegie Research 1 university, conducting more than $120 million in sponsored research annually. UMass Lowell is committed to increasing diversity in its faculty, staff, and student populations, as well as curriculum and support programs, while promoting an inclusive environment. We seek candidates who can contribute to that goal and encourage you to apply and to identify your strengths in these areas. Minimum Qualifications (Required): Candidates must hold an earned clinical doctorate in nursing (DNP), or a research doctorate in nursing (PhD) or a related field from an accredited institution Candidates must hold an active, unencumbered RN/NP license; Massachusetts RN/NP license is required prior to the start of the employment Active, national APRN certification RN/APRN experience in the United States Teaching experience in four-year University/College institutions Family nurse practitioner (FNP) with relevant clinical experience in primary care, ambulatory settings Preferred Qualifications: Experience with supervision of DNP scholarly projects Experience teaching online courses Previous regional or national presentations Previous peer reviewed publications Active involvement/leadership in national organizations Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited and a Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA union position. Formal minimum for Clinical Instructor is $75,000, however, anticipated salary range of $88,000 - $158,000, based on clinical rank and qualifications. Initial review of applications will begin on November 9, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The position may close once an adequate number of qualified candidates have been received. Please submit a CV, cover letter, teaching statement/philosophy, writing sample or publication, and names and contact information of three references. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530429/tenure-track-assistantassociatefull-professor-solomont-school-of-nursing": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Solomont School of Nursing",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant/Associate/Full Professor - Solomont School of Nursing Apply now Job no: 530429 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Solomont School of Nursing Salary: See posting section for salary details (based on rank/qualifications) Applications Open: Oct 28 2026 Applications Close: General Summary of Position: The Susan and Alan Solomont School of Nursing at University of Massachusetts Lowell seeks a full-time tenure-track (open rank) faculty member with an established program of research or solid research trajectory. Priority research areas include health promotion, the healthcare workforce, and historically excluded and underserved populations, but applicants with expertise in other areas are also encouraged to apply. Tenure-track faculty have a base teaching load of 4 courses per year, with reductions based on research productivity; generous teaching releases are standard for assistant professors in their first four years. All faculty in the school are expected to engage in service to the School, College, and University, commensurate with rank. We seek applicants recognized for scholarly productivity, ability to work with diverse populations, and excellent oral and written communication skills. The candidate may be appointed at the Assistant, Associate or Full Professor level, depending upon qualifications. A growing, dynamic, and well-regarded school established in 1968, the Solomont School of Nursing enrolls over 550 nursing students in the BS, RN to BS, Master’s, Doctor of Nursing Practice and PhD in Nursing programs and has 20 full-time faculty members. NCLEX-RN pass rates and NP certification exam pass rates consistently exceed 90%. Faculty have diverse interests and conduct research in the areas of health disparities, correctional worker health, occupational health, pediatrics, and women’s health, with many active collaborations with other units across campus, such as public health, biomedical and nutritional sciences, biomedical engineering, computer science and psychology. The Solomont School of Nursing is located within the Zuckerberg College of Health Sciences, a vibrant and growing community of 64 faculty and over 2100 students. Zuckerberg College prepares professionals and scientists in a wide range of health science fields including nursing, biomedical, pharmaceutical and nutritional sciences, physical therapy and exercise science, and public health. Faculty research within Zuckerberg College ranges from laboratory research to clinical interventions and community-based projects and there is a supportive culture for both interdisciplinary and community-based research across the College. Research programs with significant funding and strong community partnerships and engagement opportunities enrich and support our educational programs. Many faculty are part of the Center for Population Health, a vibrant university-wide research center whose home is in the College. The College is a leader in interprofessional education, research and practice opportunities for faculty and students. There are numerous opportunities for research collaborations with other UMass Lowell Colleges and Research Centers, across the 5-campus UMass system, and across the greater Boston area. The University of Massachusetts Lowell, located about 25 miles northwest of Boston in the high-tech corridor of Massachusetts, is a diverse public research university enrolling nearly 17,000 students. Approximately 48% of undergraduates identify as being from diverse backgrounds and 43% are first-generation college students. UMass Lowell has experienced dramatic growth in research funding, total endowments, and student enrollment over the past ten years. It is ranked the #1 public university in Massachusetts by the Wall Street Journal and the #1 public university in New England for return on investment. It is a Carnegie Classification Research 1 university and conducts more than $120 million in sponsored research annually. Located 30 miles north of Boston, and part of the five-campus University of Massachusetts system, faculty at UMass Lowell can easily collaborate with clinical sites and researchers in Boston, Worcester, and surrounding areas. Minimum Qualifications (Required): Candidates must hold an earned research doctorate in nursing or a related field from an accredited institution Candidates with a doctorate in a field other than nursing must have a master’s degree in nursing Candidates must hold an active RN license and a Massachusetts RN license is required prior to the start of the employment RN experience in the United States Previous and recent publication in peer-reviewed journals Teaching experience in four-year University/College institutions, including graduate teaching required for candidates seeking Associate/Full Professor Be at, OR have credentials consistent with, the rank: Candidates seeking Assistant Professor must have a beginning program of research and a clear research trajectory Candidates seeking Associate Professor must currently be at, or have credentials consistent with, tenured Associate Professor with a strong record of scholarship and recent or current extramurally supported research Candidates seeking Full Professor must currently be a tenured Associate or Full Professor with a strong record and evidence of recent scholarship and a consistent and current record of extramurally supported research Preferred Qualifications: • Post-doctoral education experience is strongly preferred for candidates seeking Assistant Professor • Editorial board experience for candidates seeking Associate/Full Professor • Active involvement/leadership in national organizations for candidates seeking Associate/Full Professor Special Instructions to Applicants: This is a benefited, Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA union position. Collective bargaining unit lists minimum for Assistant Professor as $75,000, Associate Professor as $85,000 and Professor as $100,000. However, anticipated salary range of $105,000 - $175,000, based on rank and qualifications. Initial review of applications will begin on November 15 th and continue until the position is filled. However, the position may close when an adequate number of qualified applications are received. Please include the following required documents with your application: CV Cover letter Teaching statement/philosophy to include a description of previous teaching experience Research statement, including past research accomplishments and future plan Copy of up to three recent research publications (upload publications (3) as one file) Names and contact information of three references will be required at the time of application. References will be contacted after on-campus visits. One reference must be/have been a supervisor. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530434/visiting-lecturer-mathematics-and-statistics-department": {
      "title": "Visiting Lecturer - Mathematics and Statistics Department",
      "description": "Visiting Lecturer - Mathematics and Statistics Department Apply now Job no: 530434 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Non-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Mathematics & Statistics Salary: See posting section for salary details. Applications Open: Oct 28 2026 Applications Close: General Summary of Position: The Department of Mathematics and Statistics at the University of Massachusetts Lowell invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Visiting Lecturer to start on September 1, 2027. The primary responsibility will be to provide high-quality teaching of undergraduate courses in the Department of Mathematics and Statistics. This position is renewable annually, for a maximum of four years. UMass Lowell is located about 25 miles northwest of Boston in the high-tech corridor of Massachusetts. As a Visiting Lecturer, the successful candidate will teach four classes per semester (12 credits) at the undergraduate level. Courses taught by department faculty can be found at: https://www.uml.edu/Catalog/Undergraduate/Sciences/Departments/Mathematical-Sciences/Course-Listing.aspx Applicants may apply separately to both our Assistant Teaching Professor and Visiting Lecturer posts if they meet the requirements of each. Minimum Qualifications (Required): Earned doctoral or master’s degree in mathematics, statistics, or another field that provides substantial preparation for teaching undergraduate mathematics or statistics. Evidence of successful teaching at the college or university level, including at least two years of teaching experience for doctoral degree holders or five years for master's degree holders. Relevant professional experience involving substantial use, communication, or application of mathematics or statistics may be considered in lieu of a portion of the teaching experience requirement. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Additional Considerations: Demonstrated success teaching undergraduate mathematics or statistics courses, or equivalent evidence of the ability to communicate mathematical ideas effectively to diverse student populations. Candidates with industry experience in actuarial science, data science, applied mathematics, or other fields directly relevant to teaching mathematics are particularly encouraged to apply. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, non-unit visiting lecturer position. Anticipated salary range of $95,000-$100,000, based on qualifications. Initial review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. However, the position may close when an adequate number of qualified applications is received. Please include the following required documents with your application: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy, with particular attention to strategies for promoting student learning, engagement, and accountability in undergraduate mathematics courses in an era of AI. Names and contact information of three references will be required during the application process. Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://explorejobs.uml.edu/en-us/job/530422/assistant-teaching-professor-and-coordinator-of-undergraduate-statistics-programs": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor and Coordinator of Undergraduate Statistics Programs",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor and Coordinator of Undergraduate Statistics Programs Apply now Job no: 530422 Position type: Faculty Full Time Benefit Status: Benefited-Union Campus: UMass Lowell Department: Mathematics & Statistics Salary: Please refer to the salary range in posting section. Applications Open: Oct 28 2026 Applications Close: Open until filled General Summary of Position: The Department of Mathematics and Statistics at the University of Massachusetts Lowell invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Teaching Professor to start on September 1, 2027. The primary responsibilities will be to teach undergraduate courses in Statistics or related fields, and to coordinate our Undergraduate BS Statistics and BA Actuarial Studies programs. This position is renewable annually, potentially leading to an appointment as Associate Teaching Professor following six consecutive years of outstanding performance evaluations. UMass Lowell is located about 25 miles northwest of Boston in the high-tech corridor of Massachusetts. As an Assistant Teaching Professor, the successful candidate’s responsibilities include both teaching and service. Teaching will consist of three classes per semester (9 credits) at the undergraduate level. The primary service responsibility will be to provide leadership for the Statistics and Actuarial Studies programs, including academic advising, curriculum assessment and development, coordination with faculty teaching in the programs, and outreach to industry and community partners. Information about our degree programs can be found at: https://www.uml.edu/catalog/undergraduate/sciences/departments/mathematical-sciences/mathematical-sciences-major.aspx Courses taught by department faculty can be found at: https://www.uml.edu/Catalog/Undergraduate/Sciences/Departments/Mathematical-Sciences/Course-Listing.aspx Applicants may apply separately to both our Assistant Teaching Professor and Visiting Lecturer posts if they meet the requirements of each. Minimum Qualifications (Required): Earned doctoral or master’s degree in Statistics, Mathematics, Actuarial Studies, or a closely related field. Evidence of successful teaching at the college or university level, including at least two years of teaching experience for doctoral degree holders or five years for master's degree holders. Relevant professional experience in statistics, actuarial science, mathematics, data analytics, or related areas may be considered in lieu of a portion of the teaching experience requirement. Demonstrated ability to teach undergraduate statistics courses, or evidence of appropriate preparation to do so. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Additional Considerations: Strong organizational, communication, and leadership skills. Experience coordinating academic programs, multi-section courses, student success initiatives, or related educational activities. Experience working with diverse student populations, including first-generation college students. Experience teaching or using statistical and data-analysis software such as R, Python, SAS, SPSS, or similar tools. Candidates with industry credentials such as an Associate or a Fellow of a major actuarial society are particularly encouraged to apply. Special Instructions to Applicants: This position is full-time, benefited and in the Massachusetts Society of Professors/MTA/NEA union position. The formal union contract minimum for this rank is $75,000. However, anticipated salary range of $90,000 - $100,000, based on qualifications Please include the following required documents with your application: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy, including approaches to teaching statistics and quantitative reasoning, supporting student success, and preparing students for careers and advanced study in statistics, actuarial science, mathematics, or related fields. Names and contact information of three references will be required during the application process Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    }
